const body = `<h1>10.5a — Permutations: Recursive Exhaustive Search</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Backtracking is a systematic way to explore all possibilities. You try
        something, and if it doesn't work, you undo it and try something else.
        Permutations are the simplest backtracking problem — every ordering of
        a set of items.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 1: Count the Possibilities</h3>

    <p>How many ways can you arrange the letters A, B, C? Write them all out by
    hand. How many did you get? If you added a fourth letter D, how many
    arrangements would there be? What about 10 letters?</p>

    <p>The formula: n! = n × (n-1) × (n-2) × ... × 1.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">ABC: 3! = 6 permutations
  ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA

ABCD: 4! = 24 permutations

ABCDEFG: 7! = 5040 permutations</span></code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>Why does adding ONE more letter multiply the count by (n+1)? Think about
        where the new letter can be inserted into each existing arrangement. If you
        have all arrangements of ABC, how do you build all arrangements of ABCD?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: Generate with Backtracking</h3>

    <p>The backtracking approach for permutations:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Pick an unused character</li>
        <li>Place it in the current position</li>
        <li>Recursively fill the rest</li>
        <li>When the permutation is complete (all positions filled), print it</li>
        <li>Backtrack: remove the character and try the next unused one</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Trace by hand for ABC:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="output">Step 1: place 'A' at position 0, recurse
Step 2:   place 'B' at position 1, recurse
Step 3:     place 'C' at position 2 → "ABC" ✓, backtrack
Step 2:   remove 'B', try 'C' at position 1, recurse
Step 3:     place 'B' at position 2 → "ACB" ✓, backtrack
Step 2:   done, backtrack
Step 1: remove 'A', try 'B' at position 0, recurse
...(continues)</span></code></pre>

    <p>Implement it. Use a boolean array <code>used[]</code> to track which letters
    are already placed.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Mutable State</h3>
        <p>If you use a single char array <code>perm[]</code> and fill it in-place,
        at what point do you print? If you print inside the recursive call (when
        pos == letters.length), you'll see the correct permutation. If you print
        after the recursive call returns, what will you see? Why?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Count Nodes</h3>
        <p>Add a counter to your recursion. How many recursive calls happen for
        3 letters? For 4? For 5? The number of calls is much larger than the number
        of permutations — each leaf is a permutation, but there are many intermediate
        nodes. What's the relationship?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain why backtracking undoes choices before trying alternatives</li>
            <li>Generate all permutations of a string using recursion + a used[] array</li>
            <li>Trace a backtracking algorithm by hand for a small input</li>
            <li>Explain why the number of recursive calls is much larger than n!</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/recursion-basics.html">Recursion Basics</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/trees-and-traversal.html">Trees &amp; Traversal</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
